<!-- ═══════════════════════════════ TRANSLATOR'S SUMMARY ════════════════════ -->
<div class="summation">
  <h3>Translator's Summary</h3>
  <p>
    Reb Noson reveals the deep spiritual logic behind the two kosher signs — cud-chewing
    and split hooves — and the distinction between <em>beheimah</em> (animal) and
    <em>chayah</em> (wild beast). The entire framework rests on three axes:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Speech vs. Silence:</strong> The soul in food yearns to rise to the place
    of speech (throat/mouth). In kosher animals this upward surge is the cud-chew. In
    impure animals the souls are fully trapped — "a mouth that does not speak."</li>
    <li><strong>Drunkenness and the Tree of Knowledge:</strong> Animal nature = confused
    mind = drunkenness = Tree of Knowledge of Good and Evil. In the kosher animal there is
    good mixed in; in the impure, none. Purim drunkenness is the unique moment when the
    evil side of that Tree is subdued and the wine serves only to reveal the inner good.</li>
    <li><strong>Right/Left — Chayah/Beheimah:</strong> Wild beasts come from the right
    (Avraham/Chesed) — white brain like silver — fat permitted, blood covered by love.
    Animals from the left (Yitzchak/Din) — turbid mind — fat forbidden, blood revealed.
    The horn is the mirror of the mind: its clarity or turbidity tells you everything.</li>
  </ul>
